From eaf40ffe7750f483439b7ed0236c26bbc910b1a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anees Iqbal <hello@aneesiqbal.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:17:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 46/68] vgpu: a clear-only pass publishes through its resolve
 texture, for both resolving store actions

Component: crates/reims-vgpu, runtime::exec clear rail; x86 macOS / Linux Vulkan
pathway, found on the macos-13 rail.

`apply_clear` refused `MTLStoreActionStoreAndMultisampleResolve` by name and
dropped the guest's clear, immediately above the code that already handled its
sibling correctly by retargeting the write to the resolve texture.

The contract says the two are the same here, and it is public API rather than
anything recovered: `MTLLoadActionClear` with no draws leaves **every sample** of
the attachment holding `clearColor`, so resolving those samples yields exactly
`clearColor`. Both resolve-carrying store actions therefore publish the same
single-sample result into `resolveTexture`. They differ only in whether the
multisample texture is additionally retained, which is not the surface this path
publishes and not one the guest scans out -- which is why the sibling had always
ignored that half too.

This matters more than it did last week. `apply_clear` is now the only thing that
lands a pass clear in guest memory, because the commit before this one removed the
eager pre-draw seed that used to cover the same attachments. That seed's gate was
`store_action_publishes_single_sample`, which accepts
`StoreAndMultisampleResolve`; this refusal does not. So the previous commit took
away the cover for a case that was already being dropped, and the two together
would have lost every such clear with nothing but this one line to say so.

The decision is now a predicate, `clear_publish_target` -> `ClearPublish`, so the
rule for which texture a clear lands in is stated once and can be tested without
a device. `apply_clear` consumes its answer instead of re-deriving the retarget
from `resolve_texture_ref`, which was the same rule spelled a second time. The
`Direct` case keeps the attachment exactly as the guest declared it, level and
all; only `Resolved` rewrites the target, and level zero there is a property of a
resolve target rather than a general normalisation.

`clear_multisample_resolve_target_missing` still refuses a resolve that names no
resolve texture, now for both actions and carrying the store action, so admitting
the pair above cannot silently admit that.

Found by a human opening every app in the Dock on a driven macos-13 boot, which
fired the refusal 27 times in a 16 s window on one attachment pair (`source=4
resolve=3`). No automated probe in this tree had produced it: a
sustained-animation boot of the same build refused zero draws and cleared
zero passes this way.

Verified: `cargo test -p reims-vgpu --no-default-features --features host-window
-- --test-threads=1` -- 2209 lib green, other targets green;
`wire_fixtures_reach_the_decoders` fails 2, byte-identical to master and
pre-existing. clippy `-D warnings` exits 0 on all three arms.

Gated by `a_clear_publishes_through_the_resolve_texture_for_both_resolving_store_actions`,
verified by ablation: restoring the refusal fails it with `NotPublished` against
the expected `Resolved(3)`.

Not verified at commit time: no live boot yet with this fix in the device, so the
27-refusal count has not been observed going to zero, and no frame has been
compared. No `conformance/` case: the battery does not exist on this base.
